#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# round

#% text
# `round(x)` gives the integer nearest to `x`.

#% text
# `round(x, a)` gives the multiple of `a` nearest to `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A value exactly halfway between two integers goes to the even one: `round(2.5)` is 2 and `round(3.5)` is 4.

#% item
# Exact integers, fractions, machine reals and balls give exact integers.

#% item
# A complex number is rounded in its real and imaginary parts separately.

#% item
# `round(x, a)` is `a*round(x/a)`.

#% item
# `round` is listable: it applies to each element of a list.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
round(2.4)
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
round(7/2)
#% output
#= 4

#% text
# Ties go to the even integer:

#% cell
round([0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5])
#% output
#= [0, 2, 2, 4]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Nearest multiples:

#% cell
round(1234, 100)
#% output
#= 1200

#% cell
round(17, 5)
#% output
#= 15

#% text
# Negative numbers:

#% cell
round(-2.5)
#% output
#= -2

#% text
# A complex number:

#% cell
round(2.5 + 3.5im)
#% output
#= 2 + 4im

#% text
# A ball:

#% cell
round(n(pi, 30))
#% output
#= 3

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The integer closest to `e^(pi*sqrt(163))`, from enough digits:

#% cell
round(n(e^(pi*sqrt(163)), 40))
#% output
#= 262537412640768744

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `round` lies between `floor` and `ceiling`:

#% cell
[floor(2.6), round(2.6), ceiling(2.6)]
#% output
#= [2, 3, 3]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `floor`, `ceiling`, `chop`, `rationalize`
